      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 55132.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 662,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of water in all its aspects:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is uniquely fascinating to scientists because of its unusual properties:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry vital minerals and nutrients through our bloodstreams and the soil.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps body temperatures stable.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\n* **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth’s surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is fresh water**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and ponds.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop known as the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back into bodies of water.\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\n* **Biological Necessity:** Life as we know it cannot exist without water. The human body is made up of about **60% water**, and the brain is roughly 73% water. \n* **Metabolism:** Water acts as the medium for almost all the cellular and chemical processes in living organisms. It flushes out toxins, regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, and digests food.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water creates habitats for millions of species of plants and animals, ranging from microscopic plankton to the blue whale.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a humble, odorless, tasteless, and transparent liquid, yet it is the most precious resource on Earth. It carved the Grand Canyon, powers our weather systems, fills our oceans, and courses through our veins."
